--TEST--
A cache key is the bytes the program handed over — keys differing by one byte, by case, by a space, by a combining mark or by their last character are separate entries, and two spellings of the same bytes are one — `rule:concurrency/a-cached-value-is-copied-across-the-boundary`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// § 2 fixes what happens to the *value* — the graph copy, in both directions —
// and says nothing about the key, because there is nothing to say: the key is
// the bytes it is. `crates/nvs-stdlib/src/cache.rs`'s store is a map keyed on
// those bytes and on nothing derived from them. The failure this pins is the
// plausible one, since a store that lower-cased, trimmed, normalised or
// truncated a key would answer correctly for every key asked on its own and
// hand one caller another's entry only where two keys it had decided were
// equal were not.
var $cache = Core\Cache::local();

// Every neighbouring pair below differs by exactly one of the things such a
// store would remove: one byte in the middle, one byte of case, a leading or a
// trailing space, a combining mark NFC folds away, and — for one that
// truncated — the last byte of a key far longer than any bound it might pick.
string $long = Core\Str::repeat("tenant/west/", 24);
array<string> $keys = [
    "user:1",
    "user:2",
    "user",
    "User",
    "USER",
    "user ",
    " user",
    "caf\u{e9}",
    "cafe\u{301}",
    $long . "a",
    $long . "b",
];

// The value is derived from the key so that no two are equal, which is what
// turns a collision into a wrong *answer* rather than a missing one: two keys
// the store had treated as one leave the earlier of them reading back the
// later's value, so the count drops while every line still looks plausible.
foreach ($keys as string $key) {
    $cache->put($key, "<" . $key . ">");
}
tainted string $back = "";
int $own = 0;
foreach ($keys as string $key) {
    $back = $cache->get($key) as string;
    if ($back == "<" . $key . ">") {
        $own = $own + 1;
    }
}
echo "each key answered its own value: ", $own, " of ", Core\Arr::count($keys), "\n";

// The negative half of the same claim: a key one byte away from a stored one
// was never written, and absence is the only answer a store keyed on bytes has
// for it — § 1's `null`, reached because nothing matched rather than because
// something was forgotten.
string $near = $cache->get("user:3") == null ? "absent" : "present";
echo "user:3 is ", $near, "\n";

// Case is a byte difference like any other, printed rather than counted so the
// pair the count covers is visible.
tainted string $lower = $cache->get("user") as string;
tainted string $upper = $cache->get("User") as string;
echo "user -> ", $lower, ", User -> ", $upper, "\n";

// The two spellings of one word are compared as bytes and not as text, so the
// store performs no Unicode normalisation on the way in — the answers are
// compared to each other here rather than printed, since both render alike.
tainted string $composed = $cache->get("caf\u{e9}") as string;
tainted string $decomposed = $cache->get("cafe\u{301}") as string;
string $folded = $composed == $decomposed ? "one entry" : "two entries";
echo "two spellings of one word are ", $folded, "\n";

// And a key is compared whole: two that agree for hundreds of characters and
// differ at the last one are two entries, which a store hashing a bounded
// prefix would fail while passing every short key above.
tainted string $tailA = $cache->get($long . "a") as string;
tainted string $tailB = $cache->get($long . "b") as string;
string $truncated = $tailA == $tailB ? "one entry" : "two entries";
echo "keys agreeing for ", Core\Str::length($long), " characters and differing at the last are ", $truncated, "\n";

// Differing by nothing at all is the other side of it: the store compares what
// a string holds and not which literal produced it, so a key built at run time
// reaches the entry written under an equal literal.
string $built = "us" . "er" . ":1";
tainted string $viaBuilt =$cache->get($built) as string;
echo "an equal key built at run time reads ", $viaBuilt, "\n";
--EXPECT--
each key answered its own value: 11 of 11
user:3 is absent
user -> <user>, User -> <User>
two spellings of one word are two entries
keys agreeing for 288 characters and differing at the last are two entries
an equal key built at run time reads <user:1>
